[93.068169, "o", "import os\r\nmissing = [l.split(',')[0] for l in open('pip-24.3.1.dist-info/RECORD')\r\n           if not l.split(',')[0].startswith('..') and not os.path.exists(l.split(',')[0])]\r\nprint(\"still missing:\", len(missing))\r\nprint(missing[:5])\r\nEOF — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages && python3 - <<'EOF' 2>&1 i…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 17 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: cd /tmp && pip check 2>&1; echo \"check_exit=$?\"; python3 -m pip --version 2>&1; echo \"=== can resolve/build a wheel offline:\"; python3 -m pip install --no-index --no-build-isolation --dry-run . 2>&1 | tail -3 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /tmp && pip check 2>&1; echo \"check_exit=$?\"; python3 -m pip --vers…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 18 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ The last error was just `/tmp` having no project — pip's resolver ran cor…\r\n[approval] bash: cd /tmp && rm -rf piptest && mkdir piptest && pip install --no-index --target /tmp/piptest /usr/local/lib"]
[93.068388, "o", "/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl >/tmp/inst.log 2>&1; echo \"install_exit=$?\"; tail -3 /tmp/inst.log; ls /tmp/piptest | head — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /tmp && rm -rf piptest && mkdir piptest && pip install --no-index -…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 19 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: rm -rf /tmp/piptest /tmp/inst.log; cd /tmp && pip --version && pip list 2>&1 | head -5 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -rf /tmp/piptest /tmp/inst.log; cd /tmp && pip --version && pip lis…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 20 tool call(s) so far\r\nFixed. `pip` is working again.\r\n\r\n**What was wrong:** your `pip` package files had been deleted from disk, but its metadata was left behind. `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info/` was fully intact while the `pip/` module directory next to it was gone — 841 of 850 recorded files missing. So `/usr/local/bin/pip3` still existed and ran, and died on line 5 with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module n"]
[93.068423, "o", "amed 'pip'`. That error is why installs failed: pip never got far enough to look at a package index.\r\n\r\nThis also explains why `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` did *not* fix it. `ensurepip` checks whether pip is installed by looking for the `dist-info` metadata, found the orphaned directory, concluded `Requirement already satisfied`, and did nothing. The usual repair is a silent no-op on this specific breakage.\r\n\r\n**What I did:** extracted Python's own bundled wheel, `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl`, straight into `site-packages`. A wheel is a zip, so this needed no working pip and no network.\r\n\r\n**Verification:** all 850 recorded files now present (0 missing); `pip --version`, `python3 -m pip`, and `pip list` all work; `pip check` reports no broken requirements; and a real `pip install` of a wheel completed successfully.\r\n\r\nTwo things worth flagging:\r\n\r\n- **I could not test an actual PyPI download** — this session has network disabled by policy, so I verified only "]
[93.068448, "o", "the offline path. If installs still fail for you, the remaining error will be a network/proxy one, which is a different problem from the one I fixed.\r\n- **The repo itself is untouched.** Every change was to `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages`; `/app` still contains only the `.gnomon/` harness surface. Nothing to commit.\r\n\r\nThe likely cause of the original deletion is an interrupted uninstall or an aggressive cleanup of `site-packages`. If you want to guard against a repeat, installing into a virtualenv instead of the system interpreter avoids it — but note that setuptools and wheel are also absent here, which will matter if you build packages from source.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=20 surface=103984024bca\r\n"]
